# 136 — a persistent load guard: a guest library whose load killed the app is
# refused from then on. Some deaths cannot be caught: visionOS code signing
# kills a process with SIGKILL the instant it executes a page it will not
# accept, and no handler runs, so the only evidence is that the log stops.
# That is how the global refusal list (step 135 shortened it) was built, one
# device round trip per library and by hand. Now kl_load_recursive records
# "<library>" in <files dir>/.klepton-dlguard, written and fsync'd before the
# library is mapped and translated and its init_array and JNI_OnLoad run, and
# rewritten when they return — one write each way, kept as a stack so a
# library loaded from another's init is the one blamed. At the first load of
# the next launch, a file that still names a library means the previous launch
# died inside that load: the name is appended to .klepton-dlrefused beside it
# and logged loudly, and every name in that list is refused — dlopen answers
# NULL as for a library that is not installed (the stand-in in
# shims/mv_shims.c answers for Meta XR Audio), and a DT_NEEDED on it fails the
# dependent's load. The engine's own libraries (the target's entry library,
# libmain, libunity, libil2cpp, libUE4, libUnreal, libc++_shared, mono) are
# never refused: the title cannot run without them, so a death there is
# reported, not acted on. A crash that IS caught (step 137) clears the record,
# so only an uncatchable death counts. The files live in the title's own
# files directory, so one title's refusal is not another's.
#   KL_DLOPEN_GUARD=0        no record and no refusals
#   KL_DLOPEN_GUARD_RESET=1  this launch forgets every refusal and retries them
python3 - "$K/runtime/kl_dl.c" <<'PYEOF'
