# 146 — step 145's file joins the runtime, and what its Java needs of the
# runtime's own surface:
#  - references are held. An object array keeps what SetObjectArrayElement
#    (or NewObjectArray's initial element) stored in it, a HashMap what put
#    stored, and a host object of step 145 what it keeps; each lets go when it
#    is itself retired (DeleteLocalRef, PopLocalFrame), not before. Until now a
#    container kept a bare pointer, so a guest that built a String[] or a map
#    of fresh strings and deleted its local references (correct JNI, and what
#    the Firebase C++ SDK does for every FieldPath and map key) left the
#    container holding retired objects: "expected a java/lang/String, got an
#    untagged pointer". klj_hold / klj_release are the C NewGlobalRef /
#    DeleteGlobalRef, the release retiring an object no one holds any more.
#  - `new HashMap()` made no map: NewObject hands a constructor its jclass, and
#    HashMap.<init> stored the map's payload in the class object and returned
#    the class, so every HashMap was one object and the interned class lost its
#    name. It now builds a fresh map, whose payload is freed with it.
#  - HashMap is a java.util.Map and ArrayList a java.util.List to IsInstanceOf
